WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.852
The blade passed through the block, but no useful

00:00:03.852 --> 00:00:06.876
section formed. What fell away was fragmented material.

00:00:06.876 --> 00:00:09.900
I photographed it because that was the result.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.313
Zebrafish specimens are small enough that a slight

00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:20.210
change in orientation changes which anatomical

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.142
structures the knife reaches.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:30.134
I used two-percent agarose to hold the specimen's

00:00:30.134 --> 00:00:32.897
position, intending to support the preparation

00:00:32.897 --> 00:00:34.279
afterward in paraffin.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.051
Agarose helped with orientation. Paraffin was supposed

00:00:44.051 --> 00:00:47.651
to make sectioning possible. Their jobs appeared

00:00:47.651 --> 00:00:49.195
complementary on paper.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.933
At the microtome, the assembly separated rather than

00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.981
producing an intact ribbon. The instrument encountered

00:00:59.981 --> 00:01:03.465
a block whose materials did not hold together.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.268
Their different affinities for water offer a plausible

00:01:12.268 --> 00:01:15.609
explanation, but the photograph alone cannot establish

00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:17.995
the precise mechanism of failure.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.436
A different embedding sequence, better dehydration, or

00:01:24.436 --> 00:01:27.849
a compatible support system might change the outcome.

00:01:27.849 --> 00:01:30.409
That would need a new experiment.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.984
There is no final histological section in this story.

00:01:37.984 --> 00:01:41.518
The images show exactly where the procedure ended. They

00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:45.052
also tell the next person what must be redesigned.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.692
An attractive diagram could hide that. The actual

00:01:52.692 --> 00:01:53.908
failed block cannot.
